"""Original bone chapter: treatment selection, sequencing and diagnostic pitfalls."""
from create_bone import rows,q,PT,HP,OS
from qbank_authoring import ref,finalize
GI=ref('ACR: Glucocorticoid-Induced Osteoporosis Guideline, 2022','https://doi.org/10.1002/acr.25240')
PG=ref('Paget Association: Evidence-Based Paget Guideline, 2019','https://paget.org.uk/health-and-research-professionals/clinical-guideline/')
RO=ref('Endocrine Society: Osteoporosis Guideline Update, 2020','https://academic.oup.com/jcem/article/105/3/587/5739968')
HPP=ref('GeneReviews: Hypophosphatasia, including International Working Group diagnostic criteria','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK1150/')
for item in rows:
 if 'focal bone enlargement' in item['question']:item['references'].append(PG)
q('How do nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates such as alendronate mainly reduce bone resorption?', 'They bind mineral and impair osteoclast function through inhibition of the mevalonate pathway',[
 ('They directly replace PTH as a continuous physiological hormone','They are antiresorptive medicines, not PTH replacement.'),('They activate RANKL to increase osteoclast formation','Their effect reduces resorption.'),('They work only by increasing intestinal calcium absorption','This is not their main mechanism.')],
 'Bone affinity creates a prolonged skeletal effect', '**Osteoclast uptake inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase for nitrogen-containing agents.**',['Skeletal retention explains residual effects after stopping.','Different agents have different fracture-outcome and dosing evidence.'], '**Select the agent for the fracture-risk profile and safety.**',['Check calcium, vitamin D and renal eligibility.','A drug class mechanism does not make all routes and products interchangeable.'],[['Site','Bone mineral/osteoclast'],['Effect','Reduced resorption'],['Clinical feature','Residual skeletal action']],[OS])
q('A patient starts oral alendronate. Which administration instruction is important?', 'Take with plain water on an empty stomach and remain upright for the product-specified interval',[
 ('Take immediately before lying down with minimal water','This increases esophageal injury risk.'),('Take together with calcium to maximize absorption','Calcium impairs absorption.'),('Crush into a full meal as the standard method','Food markedly reduces oral bioavailability.')],
 'Administration affects both efficacy and esophageal safety', '**Oral absorption is low and sensitive to food and other medicines.**',['Esophageal disease and inability to remain upright can make an oral route unsuitable.','The exact interval differs by product.'], '**Choose a feasible route.**',['Do not persist with unsafe administration in a frail patient.','Discuss alternatives when GI tolerance or practical barriers prevent correct use.'],[['Water','Plain water'],['Food/other medicines','Separate as directed'],['Position','Remain upright']],[OS])
q('Before IV zoledronate for osteoporosis, which assessment is particularly important?', 'Renal function and correction of hypocalcemia or significant vitamin D deficiency',[
 ('No laboratory review is needed because the route bypasses the gut','Systemic safety still matters.'),('Low calcium is desirable because the infusion corrects it','Antiresorption can worsen hypocalcemia.'),('The drug is equally suitable at every level of kidney function','Renal contraindications and product guidance apply.')],
 'Infusion convenience does not remove renal or mineral risks', '**Zoledronate has renal-function limits and can cause an acute-phase reaction.**',['Hydration and infusion practice follow the product protocol.','Hypocalcemia risk rises when mineral status is inadequate.'], '**Review dental, medication and fracture context.**',['Explain expected transient symptoms versus warning signs.','Select an alternative when renal or other contraindications apply.'],[['Kidney','Check eligibility'],['Calcium/vitamin D','Correct deficits'],['Infusion','Follow validated protocol']],[OS])
q('A lower-risk patient has completed five years of oral bisphosphonate therapy without new fracture. What is the best next principle?', 'Reassess fracture risk and consider a monitored treatment pause if appropriate',[
 ('Every patient must stop at five years regardless of fracture risk','Higher-risk patients often need continued treatment.'),('No reassessment is needed until a fracture occurs','Duration decisions require active review.'),('Apply the same holiday rule to denosumab','Denosumab discontinuation has different risks.')],
 'Bisphosphonate persistence permits selected pauses', '**NOGG recommends reassessment after about five years oral or three years IV therapy.**',['Age, prior major fracture, glucocorticoids and new fractures favor longer treatment.','Residual effect differs by agent.'], '**Plan follow-up and restart criteria.**',['Reassess after a new fracture or risk change.','A holiday is a monitored option rather than permanent abandonment.'],[['Oral course','Reassess around five years'],['IV course','Reassess around three years'],['Pause','Selected lower-risk patients']],[OS])
q('Which target is inhibited by denosumab in osteoporosis?', 'RANKL',[
 ('Sclerostin','That is the target of romosozumab.'),('The calcium-sensing receptor as an agonist','That describes a different drug mechanism.'),('The TSH receptor','This is unrelated to its bone action.')],
 'Blocking osteoclast formation reduces resorption', '**RANKL inhibition prevents osteoclast development and activity.**',['The effect is reversible when dosing stops.','Hypocalcemia risk requires attention, particularly in advanced CKD.'], '**Plan continuity before starting.**',['Do not delay scheduled doses casually.','Ensure an exit strategy and mineral safety monitoring.'],[['Denosumab','RANKL inhibition'],['Romosozumab','Sclerostin inhibition'],['Key distinction','Different discontinuation physiology']],[OS])
q('A patient wants to stop denosumab after several years because BMD improved. What is the essential next step?', 'Arrange an appropriate sequential antiresorptive plan rather than an unprotected stop',[
 ('Use the same drug-holiday approach as a bisphosphonate','Bone turnover can rebound after denosumab.'),('No treatment is needed once one DXA improves','Rebound vertebral fractures can occur.'),('Stop all follow-up because the original risk is permanently cured','Risk and treatment effects require reassessment.')],
 'A reversible antiresorptive effect can rebound', '**Denosumab cessation can cause rapid bone loss and multiple vertebral fractures.**',['A subsequent antiresorptive is needed when treatment is discontinued.','Kidney function and prior exposure affect the choice and timing.'], '**Make the transition plan before the next dose is missed.**',['Use specialist input when standard options are contraindicated.','Do not allow administrative delays to create an unintended gap.'],[['Problem','Rebound turnover'],['Harm','Rapid loss/vertebral fractures'],['Plan','Sequential antiresorptive']],[OS],'clinical_scenario')
q('Which mechanism is characteristic of romosozumab?', 'Sclerostin inhibition with increased bone formation and reduced resorption',[
 ('RANKL blockade as its specific antibody target','That describes denosumab.'),('Direct PTH-receptor replacement throughout life','It is not a PTH replacement product.'),('Calcium chelation from bone','This is not its therapeutic action.')],
 'A bone-signaling inhibitor can produce dual remodeling effects', '**Romosozumab can provide rapid fracture-risk reduction in selected very-high-risk patients.**',['Treatment is time limited.','Cardiovascular risk and mineral status affect suitability.'], '**Follow with antiresorptive therapy.**',['Maintain gains after the course.','Choose the sequence as part of the original treatment plan.'],[['Target','Sclerostin'],['Effect','Formation increase/resorption decrease'],['After course','Antiresorptive consolidation']],[OS,RO])
q('A postmenopausal woman with very high fracture risk had a recent MI. What issue particularly affects romosozumab selection?', 'Its cardiovascular warning makes recent MI a major reason to avoid starting it and choose another strategy',[
 ('The drug is preferred specifically because it treats coronary thrombosis','It is not a cardiovascular treatment.'),('Fracture risk automatically overrides every cardiovascular contraindication','Benefit-risk and product restrictions still apply.'),('No alternative osteoporosis treatments exist','Other anabolic or antiresorptive options may be suitable.')],
 'Very high skeletal risk does not remove nonskeletal safety constraints', '**Product labeling and guidelines caution against use in patients with relevant MI or stroke risk.**',['The exact regulatory wording varies by jurisdiction.','Review the timing and overall cardiovascular profile.'], '**Select a safe alternative and retain a sequencing plan.**',['Do not leave severe fracture risk untreated merely because one agent is unsuitable.','Coordinate specialist care when risks compete.'],[['Benefit','Fracture protection'],['Concern','Cardiovascular warning'],['Action','Alternative risk-appropriate therapy']],[RO,OS],'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has multiple recent vertebral fractures and very low BMD. Which strategy should be considered early?', 'An anabolic-first approach in an appropriate very-high-risk patient, followed by antiresorptive therapy',[
 ('Calcium alone is equivalent to fracture-reducing medication','It is not sufficient for this risk level.'),('Wait for another fracture to establish importance','Recent multiple fractures signal high near-term risk.'),('Every patient must use the same lowest-intensity option first','Risk-based treatment can justify a different sequence.')],
 'Near-term fracture risk influences treatment intensity', '**Anabolic therapy can be appropriate for selected very-high-risk disease.**',['Available agents and contraindications determine the choice.','Recent fractures create an opportunity for rapid secondary prevention.'], '**Plan the full sequence and practical delivery.**',['Follow with antiresorptive treatment to preserve gains.','Assess falls, nutrition and secondary causes concurrently.'],[['Risk','Multiple recent vertebral fractures'],['Consideration','Anabolic first'],['Next phase','Antiresorptive maintenance']],[OS,RO],'clinical_scenario')
q('After a course of teriparatide, what is generally needed to retain skeletal benefit?', 'Subsequent antiresorptive treatment when appropriate',[
 ('No further treatment or risk review can be needed','Bone gains can be lost without consolidation.'),('A fixed denosumab holiday regardless of prior therapy','Treatment transitions must follow the actual drug sequence.'),('A large glucocorticoid dose to lock in bone formation','This would harm bone.')],
 'Anabolic gains require a maintenance strategy', '**Stopping bone-forming therapy without follow-on treatment can permit loss of benefit.**',['The choice depends on renal function, risk and prior medicines.','This sequencing principle also applies to other time-limited anabolic courses.'], '**Make the next step before the course ends.**',['Review DXA, fracture history and adherence.','Avoid gaps caused by assuming the course itself is a permanent cure.'],[['Anabolic phase','Builds benefit'],['After cessation','Gains can decline'],['Consolidation','Antiresorptive strategy']],[OS,RO])
q('A patient with severe osteoporosis previously received external-beam radiation involving the skeleton. What is relevant before choosing a PTH analog?', 'The history raises an important product-specific safety concern and requires an alternative or specialist risk review',[
 ('Prior skeletal radiation is irrelevant to PTH-analog selection','It is a recognized precaution/avoidance context.'),('A PTH analog is mandatory regardless of cancer history','Selection remains individualized.'),('All antiresorptives are automatically prohibited by this history','Different classes have different restrictions.')],
 'Anabolic selection includes the bone malignancy and radiation history', '**PTH analogs are generally avoided in patients with relevant skeletal radiation or increased osteosarcoma risk.**',['Paget disease and skeletal malignancy are other important considerations.','Current product wording should be checked.'], '**Treat fracture risk with a suitable alternative.**',['Review the original cancer and field with specialists.','Do not assume a high-risk patient has no options.'],[['History','Skeletal radiation'],['Drug issue','PTH-analog safety'],['Plan','Alternative or expert review']],[OS,RO],'clinical_scenario')
q('How should medication-related osteonecrosis of the jaw risk be discussed before osteoporosis-dose antiresorptive therapy?', 'Explain that it is rare at osteoporosis doses, address oral health and balance the risk against fracture prevention',[
 ('It is inevitable in every treated patient','This greatly overstates risk.'),('Dental symptoms should be ignored because the complication is rare','Prompt assessment remains appropriate.'),('Stop denosumab abruptly for any dental visit without a plan','Unplanned cessation can create vertebral-fracture risk.')],
 'Communicate absolute context rather than a frightening label alone', '**Risk differs from high-dose oncology regimens.**',['Poor oral health and invasive procedures can affect risk.','Routine dental care remains valuable.'], '**Coordinate dental and prescribing teams for complex decisions.**',['Do not impose a universal holiday across different drug mechanisms.','Maintain a fracture-safe transition if treatment changes.'],[['Osteoporosis dosing','Low absolute risk'],['Prevention','Oral health and dental review'],['Treatment changes','Drug-specific planning']],[OS])
q('Persistent thigh or groin pain develops during long-term potent antiresorptive therapy. What complication should be assessed?', 'An incomplete atypical femoral fracture',[
 ('The pain is always harmless muscle soreness','It can precede a complete fracture.'),('A normal calcium excludes femoral stress injury','The diagnosis is structural.'),('Only the painful side ever needs consideration','Bilateral lesions can occur.')],
 'A rare adverse event has a recognizable prodrome', '**Atypical femoral fractures often involve the subtrochanteric or femoral shaft region.**',['Imaging and orthopaedic assessment can prevent progression.','The event is uncommon relative to ordinary fractures prevented in suitable high-risk patients.'], '**Review the drug and the overall bone plan.**',['Assess the contralateral femur when indicated.','Do not substitute reassurance for evaluation of persistent focal pain.'],[['Prodrome','Thigh/groin pain'],['Concern','Incomplete atypical fracture'],['Assessment','Imaging and bilateral consideration']],[OS],'clinical_scenario')
q('An adult is starting prednisone at least 2.5 mg/day for more than three months. What does the ACR GIOP guideline recommend?', 'Early clinical fracture-risk assessment with BMD and vertebral assessment as appropriate',[
 ('Wait for the first fracture before assessing bone','Risk evaluation should be proactive.'),('No risk exists below 20 mg/day','Lower sustained doses can matter.'),('FRAX is automatically valid at every age including children','Age limits and the clinical context apply.')],
 'Glucocorticoid fracture risk begins early', '**The 2022 guideline covers adults initiating or continuing this exposure threshold.**',['Assessment includes prior symptomatic or silent fractures.','FRAX is used from age 40 where appropriate and adjusted for dose context.'], '**Treat according to risk rather than steroid exposure alone.**',['Consider nutrition, falls and the lowest effective steroid dose.','Some high- or very-high-risk patients need prompt pharmacotherapy.'],[['Exposure','At least 2.5 mg/day for over three months'],['Timing','Assess early'],['Tools','Clinical history, BMD and vertebral assessment']],[GI])
q('Why can a glucocorticoid-treated patient fracture at a higher BMD than a person with uncomplicated postmenopausal osteoporosis?', 'Glucocorticoids impair bone quality and formation as well as bone quantity',[
 ('Glucocorticoids only change DXA measurement without affecting strength','They have real skeletal effects.'),('A T-score above −2.5 excludes all steroid-related fracture risk','BMD does not capture the whole risk.'),('Fall risk is always zero during steroid treatment','Muscle and other effects can contribute.')],
 'Density is an incomplete representation of skeletal strength', '**Glucocorticoids suppress bone formation and alter remodeling.**',['Early loss can be rapid.','Dose, duration, age and prior fracture modify risk.'], '**Use the full GIOP assessment.**',['Do not wait for a densitometric threshold alone in a high-risk patient.','Review steroid-sparing treatment and physical function.'],[['Bone quantity','Can decline'],['Bone quality','Also impaired'],['Risk assessment','Beyond DXA alone']],[GI,OS])
q('Which vitamin D test generally assesses body vitamin D status?', 'Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D',[
 ('Serum 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D in every routine deficiency assessment','It can be normal or high in deficiency due to PTH stimulation.'),('Serum calcitonin','This is a different hormone.'),('TSH','This assesses a different endocrine axis.')],
 'The storage marker and active hormone answer different questions', '**25-hydroxyvitamin D is the usual status marker.**',['Calcitriol is regulated by PTH, phosphate, FGF23 and kidney function.','Specific disorders can justify measuring the active metabolite.'], '**Test when the result will inform management.**',['Avoid indiscriminate repeated testing in low-risk people.','Interpret deficiency within the clinical and biochemical picture.'],[['Status marker','25-hydroxyvitamin D'],['Active hormone','1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D'],['Testing','Indication directed']],[OS,HP])
q('Vitamin D deficiency and bone symptoms persist despite reported adequate oral replacement after malabsorptive surgery. What should be assessed?', 'Adherence, formulation, intake and ongoing malabsorption with a tailored replacement strategy',[
 ('The deficiency is impossible if any supplement was prescribed','Prescription does not guarantee absorption.'),('Escalate indefinitely without checking calcium or response','Unmonitored treatment can be unsafe.'),('All symptoms prove isolated osteoporosis','Mineralization and nutritional disorders can coexist.')],
 'The delivered dose is not the absorbed dose', '**Malabsorptive anatomy can impair fat-soluble vitamin uptake.**',['Calcium and other nutritional deficits can contribute.','Secondary PTH elevation can reflect inadequate calcium or vitamin D.'], '**Use a monitored bariatric/nutrition plan.**',['Check calcium, phosphate, PTH and relevant nutritional markers.','Choose formulation and dosing according to the actual mechanism.'],[['Possible barrier','Malabsorption'],['Other issues','Adherence and combined deficiencies'],['Response','Tailored monitored replacement']],[OS,HP],'clinical_scenario')
q('Recurrent metatarsal stress fractures and premature tooth loss occur with persistently low ALP. What diagnosis should be considered before prescribing a bisphosphonate?', 'Hypophosphatasia',[
 ('Paget disease as the typical low-ALP process','Active Paget disease usually raises ALP.'),('Ordinary high-turnover osteoporosis is proven','The low ALP and dental phenotype suggest another disorder.'),('Vitamin D deficiency always lowers ALP profoundly','Osteomalacia from vitamin D deficiency commonly raises ALP.')],
 'Low ALP can be a clue to a mineralization enzyme defect', '**ALPL-related tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase deficiency impairs mineralization.**',['Other causes of low ALP must be excluded.','Elevated enzyme substrates and genetics can support diagnosis.'], '**Avoid reflex antiresorptive treatment of a misclassified mineralization disorder.**',['Bisphosphonates can be unsuitable in hypophosphatasia.','Seek a specialist metabolic-bone evaluation.'],[['Clues','Low ALP, stress fractures and dental history'],['Mechanism','ALPL-related enzyme deficiency'],['Implication','Different from routine osteoporosis']],[OS],'clinical_scenario')
q('Lumbar DXA appears unusually high in an older adult with marked degenerative spinal changes, but hip density is low. What is the likely issue?', 'Degenerative artifacts can falsely elevate lumbar BMD',[
 ('A high spine value guarantees all bones are strong','Artifact can mask low bone mass.'),('The hip result must be discarded automatically','Another valid site can be informative.'),('DXA cannot ever be affected by anatomy or calcification','Areal measurements are susceptible to artifacts.')],
 'Image quality is part of interpretation', '**Osteophytes and other structural changes can increase measured spine density.**',['Use valid vertebrae and alternative sites according to densitometry standards.','Unexpected changes require review of the scan rather than automatic treatment conclusions.'], '**Assess fracture risk with the reliable data.**',['Look for vertebral fractures when clinically indicated.','Use the same appropriate facility and technique for monitoring.'],[['Problem','Degenerative spine artifact'],['Effect','Apparent BMD elevation'],['Response','Review valid sites and images']],[OS],'clinical_scenario')
q('Which intervention remains important after starting effective osteoporosis medication in an older adult with recurrent falls?', 'A multifactorial falls plan including balance/strength work, medication review and environmental assessment',[
 ('Medication eliminates all fall-related fracture risk','Drug therapy does not prevent every fall.'),('Avoid all movement permanently','Deconditioning can worsen risk.'),('Use calcium alone instead of assessing vision or orthostasis','Multiple modifiable factors contribute.')],
 'Fracture prevention acts on bone strength and trauma exposure', '**Balance, muscle function, vision and postural blood pressure affect falls.**',['Sedatives and hypoglycemia-causing regimens can contribute.','Home hazards and suitable aids matter.'], '**Use an individualized practical plan.**',['Exercise should be safe and appropriate to fracture and mobility status.','Coordinate rehabilitation and bone treatment rather than treating them as alternatives.'],[['Bone','Appropriate medication'],['Falls','Strength, balance and causes'],['Environment','Hazard and aid review']],[OS])

for item in rows:
 if 'persistently low ALP' in item['question']:item['references'].append(HPP)
